<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  **The doctype has to be the first thing in the file, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy.**
  Installer sniffs the pane's *data* to decide whether it is HTML or plain text, and a file starting
  with a comment fails that sniff -- so the whole page was shown as raw markup, this comment
  included. Reproduced outside Installer with the same Cocoa importer:
  `textutil -stdin -convert txt -stdout < this-file` echoes the source, and echoes the prose once a
  doctype is in front of it. `mime-type="text/html"` in the Distribution does not rescue it.
  tools/platform/macos/installer.sh asserts the first line on every build.
  The pane Installer shows before the component list. tools/platform/macos/installer.sh stages it into the
  resources directory productbuild is given; nothing here is substituted.

  **It says only what the reader would otherwise have to find out.** An unsigned or signed-only
  build puts an obstacle in front of somebody -- a double-click reporting that the file "cannot be
  opened" -- so those two states are explained, and the placeholder below is where that explanation
  lands. A notarized build puts no obstacle there and substitutes nothing: telling somebody that a
  thing which behaves normally is going to behave normally is noise, and a pane of reassurance is
  the pane people stop reading.

  The administrator password is not mentioned for the same reason. Every installer that writes to
  /Applications asks for one, Installer's own UI is what asks, and a heading about it says nothing a
  reader did not already expect. Where a password *is* worth a sentence is the uninstaller below,
  which is a thing somebody runs by hand and can be surprised by.
-->
<html>
<head><meta charset="utf-8"></head>
<body style="font: 13px -apple-system, sans-serif; margin: 0">

<p><b>KaraokeMachine</b> &mdash; a karaoke machine, the tool that builds its song packages, a
remote for this computer, and the command-line tools. The next page lets you leave any of them
out.</p>

@SIGNING@

<h4>Removing it later</h4>

<p>Open <b>/usr/local/karaokemachine</b> and double-click
<b>Uninstall KaraokeMachine</b>. It shows you what it is about to remove, asks, and then asks for
your password &mdash; in that order. From a terminal,
<code>sudo /usr/local/karaokemachine/uninstall.sh</code> does the same thing without the questions.</p>

<p>Either way it leaves your songs, settings and catalog alone, and prints where they are on its
way out.</p>

</body>
</html>
